/*
You need to create a MovingAverage class that receives numbers one at a time. This continuous arrival of numbers is called a stream.
The class maintains a window containing only the most recent size numbers.
For every call to:

next(val)

you must:
1. Add val to the window.
2. If the window contains more than size numbers, remove the oldest number.
3. Calculate the average of the numbers currently in the window.
4. Return the average as a double.
Example
Suppose the object is initialized with:

MovingAverage obj = new MovingAverage(3);

This means the window can contain at most three numbers.
First call

obj.next(1);

Only one number has been received:

Window: [1]
Average: 1 / 1 = 1.0

Return:

1.0

Second call

obj.next(10);

Two numbers have been received:

Window: [1, 10]
Average: (1 + 10) / 2 = 5.5

Return:

5.5

Because fewer than three numbers have arrived, calculate the average using the two available numbers.
Third call

obj.next(3);

The window is now full:

Window: [1, 10, 3]
Average: (1 + 10 + 3) / 3 = 4.66667

Return:

4.66667

Fourth call

obj.next(5);

The window already contains three numbers. Remove the oldest number, 1, and add 5:

Old window: [1, 10, 3]
New window: [10, 3, 5]

Average:

(10 + 3 + 5) / 3 = 6.0

Return:

6.0

The returned results are:

[1.0, 5.5, 4.66667, 6.0]

The main idea is that the moving window always contains the most recent size values.
*/

class MovingAverage {
    int size;
    Queue<Integer> queue;
    int sum;
    public MovingAverage(int size) {
        this.size = size;

        /* Queue is an interface, so you cannot create an object directly from it:
        this.queue = new Queue<>(); // Invalid
        LinkedList is a class that implements the Queue interface. Therefore, it can behave like a FIFO queue:
        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        Here:
        Queue<Integer>  → Variable/interface type
        LinkedList<>    → Actual object implementing the queue */

        this.queue = new LinkedList<Integer>();
        this.sum=0;
    }
    public double next(int val) {

        sum += val;
        queue.offer(val);
        if(queue.size() > size)
        {
            int old = queue.remove();
            sum = sum - old ;
        }

        return (double)sum/queue.size();

    }
}
